# GC218 I have a swelling in the neck, Neck mass <span class="tag" tag-name="Surgery"><span class="smallcaps">Surgery</span></span> <span class="tag" tag-name="Lecture"><span class="smallcaps">Lecture</span></span>

- **DDx of neck mass:**
  - Benign - congenital, infective
  - Malignant - primary, secondary
- **DDx of supraclavicular fossa mass:**
  - LN metastasis - consider Lung, Breast, GI primary (if left)
  - Other causes of LN enlargement
- **DDx of lateral neck mass:**
  - Upper neck - salivary gland pathology, ?LN
  - Lower neck (under SCM) - LN, inflammatory, or neoplastic
  - Other lesions - branchial cleft cysts, carotid body tumour, neurofibroma
- **DDx of central neck mass:**
  - Lower neck - lesions from the thyroid gland
  - Upper neck - thyroglossal cyst
  - ?LN in Level 6 for thyroid cancer
- **Salient points of Hx:**
  - Demographic - age
  - HPI:
    - Site - anterior vs posterior, superior vs inferior, left vs right
    - Onset - rate of growth
    - Associated Sx:
      - 
- **P/E** - describing lumps and mumps:
  - Location
  - Size
  - Surface
  - Consistency
  - Edge
  - Tenderness
  - Mobility
  - Pulsation
  - Transillumination
- **Ix:**
  - Fine needle aspiration cytology (FNAC)
  - Endoscopic examination - identify site of possible primary tumour in upper aerodigestive tract +/- Bx
  - Imaging - XR, CT, MRI, angiography, PET-CT

# GC227 Cervical Spine Pathology <span class="tag" tag-name="Surgery"><span class="smallcaps">Surgery</span></span> <span class="tag" tag-name="Lecture"><span class="smallcaps">Lecture</span></span>

## Imaging of the cervical spine

- Views - Orthogonal views (includes AP and lateral views) on flexion and extension
- **Approach to interpretation of Cervical X-Ray:**
  - Patient details, date taken
  - Views
  - Coverage and adequacy
  - Systematic interpretation of cervical X-ray
- **Coverage and adequacy:**
  - Ensure coverage from skull-base to C7/T1 junction (C7/T1 junction common site of injury due to transition from flexible C-spine to rigid T-spine)
  - Swimmer's view by abduction and flexion of shoulder for evaluation of C7/T1 junction
- **Systematic interpretation of cervical X-Ray:**
  - Allignment
  - Bone (height, contour, fractures, radio-opacity)
  - Collapse of vertebrae
  - Disc space
  - End plates
  - Facet joints
- **Alligment** - detection of olisthesis:
  - **Interpretation of allignment** - ensure continuity of 4 lines:
    - <u>Anterior vertebral line</u> - anterior aspect of VB
    - <u>Posterior vertebral line</u> - posterior aspect of VB
    - <u>Spinolaminar line</u> - posterior extent of spinal canal
    - <u>Spinous process tip</u> - along spinous processes
  - **Causes of broken allignment** - olisthesis, which may be:
    - Degenerative
    - Traumatic
- **Measurement of cervical alignment** - to detect abnormal kyphosis or lordosis:
  - <u>Required films</u> - lateral weight-bearing films (e.g. standing or sitting)
  - <u>Technique</u> - measure C2-C7 angle, which is angle between:
    - Perpendicular line from inferior endplate of C2
    - Perpendicular line from inferior endplate of C7
  - <u>Normal cervical lordosis</u> - around 15-20%
- **Bone evaluation** - evaluation of height, contour and radio-opacity of each vertebrae:
  - <u>Bone height</u> - each cervical VB equal height except for C2 dens (which is elongated)
  - <u>Bone contour and radio-opacity</u> - detection of destructive lesions
- **Disc space evaluation** - assessment of the height of disc spaces on lateral +/- facilitation by oblique view:
  - **Lateral view** - assessment of <u>disc space</u>:
    - In normal view, disc between each vertebrae should be roughly equal
    - Disc space narrowing or collapse may arise from focal pathology
  - **Oblique view** - assessment of <u>neuroforamina</u> if evident disc-space collapse to account for Sx
- **Paravertebral soft tissue evaluation** - evaluation of <u>paravertebral soft tissue swelling</u>:
  - **Normal paravertebral soft tissue thickness:**
    - <u>At level of C2</u> - 6mm, or \< 1/2 vertebral depth
    - <u>At level of C6</u> - 22mm, or \< 1 vertebral depth
- **Assessment of cervical stenosis** - causes of narrowing of the cervical canal:
  - <u>Mid sagittal diameter</u> - distance between posterior surface of VB and anterior surface of lamina
  - <u>Dynamic stenosis</u> - distance between popstero-inferior corner of VB, to anterosuperior edge of lamina
  - <u>Olisthesis</u> - loss of continuity of the posterior vertebral line
- **Roles of dynamic C-spine views** - obtain flexion and extension views to assess dynamic instability:
  - <u>Translation</u> - \> 3.5 mm of anterior-posterior movement between vertebrae is considered unstable
  - <u>Angulation</u> - \> 11 degree change in angulation between adjacent vertebrae is abnormal
  - <u>Assessment of stenosis</u> - narrowing on dyamic views may indicate risk of atlantoaxial instability
- **Cervical X-rays:**
  - Ensure:
    - Coverage/ adequacy - skull base to C7/T1 junction (typical site of injury due to transition from flexible C-spine to rigid T-spine)
      - Pulled shoulder (Swimmer's) view - abduct and flex shoulder to visualise C7-T1 junction
    - Alignment - assessed through lateral view, ensuring continuity of following 4 lines:
      - Anterior ertebral line - anterior aspect of VB
      - Posterior vertebral line - posterior aspect of VB
      - Spinolaminar line - posterior extent of spinal canal
      - Spinous process tip - along spinous processes
      - Broken aligment - due to slip, i.e. olisthesis (due to degenerative or traumatic causes)
    - Bone evaluation - height, shape and radio-opacity of each vertebrae (should be similar except height of C2 dens, which is elongated)
    - Disc space evaluation - height
      - Oblique view - assesment of neuroforamina heights, assessment of foraminal narrowing secondary to decreased disc height or uncovertebral joint hypertrophy, disc space
    - Soft tissue evaluation - paravertebral soft tissue:
      - C2 - 6mm or \< 1/2 vertebral depth
      - C6 - 22mm or \< 1 vertebral depth
    - Measurement of cervical allignment - normal lordosis of 15-20 degrees:
      - Requires weight-bearing films - e.g. sitting or standing films
      - Inferior end plate of C2 and Inferior endplate of C7
    - Measurement of cervical stenosis and instability:
      - Assumption - normal cord is 10 mm
      - Mid-saggital diameter - relative stenosis if \< 12mm, absolute stenosis if \< 10mm
      - Dynamic stenosis (taken on extension) - distance from from posteroinferior corner of cranial vertebra to anterior superior edge of lamella (dynamic stenosis if \< 12 mm)
      - Olisthesis - slip
    - Measurement of dynamic instability - \> 3.5mm/ 11 degree change between adjacent vertebrae
  - Dynamic views
  - Assess relationship between C0/C1/C2:
    - C1/C2 relationship
      - Antlanto-dental interval - posterior to C1 arch to anterior C2 dens (increase of \< 4mm upon flexion otherwise dynamic instability)
      - Space available for the cord - C2 dens to VB of C1 (should be \>14mm)
      - Causes of C1 and C2 instability - RA, Down's
    - C0/C1 relationship - Basilar invagination (dens moves up and impinges on brainstem and spinal cord)
      - Chamberlains line - from hard palate to opisthion, i.e. posterior extent of foramen magnum (dens should not pass)
      - McGregor line - posterior part of hard palate to caudal point of occipital curve (dens should be \> 4.5mm)

## Cervical myelopathy

- **Definition** - compression of the spinal cord at the level of the cervical spine
- **Disease mechanisms resulting in cervical canal stenosis** - different parts of the vertebral column implicated:
  - <u>Vertebral body</u> - osteophyte formation (uncovertebral hypertrophy), spondylolisthesis
  - <u>Vertebral disc</u> - disc herniation
  - <u>Posterior longitudinal ligaments</u> - hypertrophy of the PLL, ossification of the PLL
  - <u>Ligamentum flavum</u> - hypertrophy of the ligamentum flavum, ossification of ligamentum flavum
- **Ossification of posterior longitudinal ligaments:**
  - <u>Definition</u> - ectopic calcification as a cause of cervical myelopathy
  - <u>Epidemiology</u>:
    - Common in Asian populations
    - Male preponderance and occurs in younger patients
- **Principles of clinical manifestation in cervical myelopathy:**
  - **Pain** - not a predominant feature
  - **Motor manifestations** - at the upper limb and lower-limb
    - LMN S/S at the level of compression
    - UMN S/S below the level of compression
  - **Sensory manifestations:**
    - <u>Loss of fine touch function</u> - primarily in the UL or fingers due to compression of the ingoing dorsal nerve root
    - <u>Loss of propioception</u> - compression of the dorsal column resulting in unsteadiness
  - **Sphincteric manifestations** - dysfunction only occurs in late stages
- **Clinical presentation in cervical myelopathy:**
  - **Clinical features relating to sensory Sx:**
  - **Clinical features relating to muscle weakness:**
  - **Unsteady, spastic gait** - as a result of loss of proprioception due to dorsal column involvement and LL spasticity
- **Natural Hx of CSM** - progressive deteriorating disorder:
  - <u>Stepwise progression</u> - occurs in 75% of patients
  - <u>Slow and steady progression</u> - occurs in 20% of patients
  - <u>RApid onset w/ stable plateau</u> - occurs in 5% of patients
- **Signs of cervical myelopathy:**
  - **Sensory disturbance:**
  - **Myelopathic hand signs:**
    - **Hoffman's sign** (C8) - stretch reflex of the FDP occuring at C8 spinal level:
      - <u>Technique</u> - wrist and MCP in hyperflexion, and passively stretches FDP and flick on middle finger
        - Rest wrist and hyperextend MCP to passively stretch FDP
        - Flick on middle finger and observe for flexion of index finger and adduction of thumb

        
        ![](~/Documents/notes/img/hoffman_sign.png)
      - <u>Interpretation</u> - flexion of thumb and index finger indicates dis-inhibition of C8 reflex (lesion is above C8)
    - **Finger escape sign:**
      - <u>Technique</u> - ask patient to present arms w/ fingers forward and palms down
      - <u>Interpretation</u> - deviation of ulnar 2 or 3 fingers due to deficient adduction 
      ![](~/Documents/notes/img/finger_escape_sign.png)
    - **Grip and release test:**
      - <u>Technique</u> - record number of grip and release actions within 10 seconds
      - <u>Interpretation</u> - normally can achieve 20 cycles:
        - Difficult, slow and incomplete finger extension
        - Exagerrated movements
  - **Long-tract signs:**
    - <u>UMN signs of the lower limb</u> - clonus, lower-limb hyper-reflexia, upgoing babinski
    - <u>Signs of DCML</u> - +ve Romberg after closing eyes, difficulty tandem walking, Lhermitte sign
  - **Sphincteric function:**
- **Modified JOA score** (Japanese Orthopaedics Association score):
  - <u>Criterion</u> - based on scoring categories for:
    - Upper limb motor function
    - Upper limb sensory function
    - Lower limb function
    - Sphincteric function
  - <u>Role</u>:
    - Assessment of disease severity and progression
    - Guides Mx
- **Ix** - for 1) evaluation of pathological mechanisms, 2) degree of compression of cord, 3) r/o intramedullary lesions, and 4) surgical planning:
  - **MRI spine:**
    - <u>Role</u>:
      - Confirm level of lesion
      - Evaluation of pathanatomy (e.g. discs, osteophytes, OPLL, flavum)
    - <u>Associated findings</u>:
      - Obliteration of CSF space
      - Myelomalacia (intramedullary signal change)
  - **Plain CT** - to delineate OPLL
  - **Dynamic X-rays** - to detect dynamic instability (e.g. C1/2 subluxation in RA)
- **Mx:**
  - **Principles of Mx:**
    - <u>Conservative Mx</u> - if no absolute indications for surgery, although no good conservative treatment options
    - <u>Surgery</u> - various surgical modalities and approaches dependent on various factors
  - **Indications of surgery:**
    - Progressive neurological impairment
    - Significant impaired ADL
    - Compatible imaging findings
  - **Surgical Mx:**
    - **Approaches:**
      - <u>Anterior approach</u> - cervical discectomy and corpectomy +/- struct reconstruction
      - <u>Posterior approach</u> - laminectomy or laminoplsaty (decompression or reconstruction of lamina)
    - **General conditions w/ assessing approach:**
      - <u>Alignment</u> - kyphotic deformities favours anterior aproach
      - <u>Pathoanatomy</u>:
        - Disc herniation better treated w/ anterior approach
        - Hypertrophy of flavum better treated w/ posterior approach
      - <u>Number of levels involved</u> - 3 level pathology favours posterior approach
      - <u>Subluxation or instability</u> - fusion is indicated
      - <u>Neck pain</u> - posterior approach is a/w more post-op axial neck pain
    - **Complications of anterior approach:**
      - Esophageal injury
      - Airway compromise
      - Vertebral artery injury
      - Pseudoarthrosis
      - Adjacent level degeneration
    - **Complications of posterior approach:**
      - Post-op kyphosis
      - C5 nerve root paresis
      - Axial Sx (e.g. shoulder and neck pain)
- Clinical features of cervical myelopathy:
  - Sensory deficits - numbness or sensory disurbance of the fingers +/- upper limb
  - Motor functions:
    - Loss of hand dexterity
    - Spastic gait
  - Poor proprioception (poor balance and easy falls)
  - Motor weakness and sphincteric dysfunction - appears only in late stage
  - Pain not a predominant feature
- Signs:
  - Long tract signs - DCML (Romberg +ve, Lhermitte, Tandem walking), Corticospinal tracts
  - Sphincter function - urinary Sx (frequency, retention), altered bowel habits
  - Functional assessment - Japanese Orthopaedic Association score (read up), guides disease severity, decision making for surgery and monitoring disease progression/ recovery
  - UMN below level of compression:
    - Myelopathic hand signs - Hoffman's sign, 10s test, Finger escape sign
    - Lower limb spasticity + UMN features
  - LMN sign at the level of compression
  - +ve Romberg and failed tandem walking
  - Hoffman's sign (C8) - stretch reflex of flexor digitorum profundus:
  - Finger escape sign - present arms with fingers forward and palms down in full adduction of fingers
    - +ve - Deficient adduction and/or extension of ulnar fingers
    - Grading from 0-4
  - 10s test - number of complete cycles of movement within 10s
    - Difficult, slow and incomplete finger extension
    - Cutoff - 20 times
  - Inverted supinator reflex (C3):
    - C5/6
    - Two components of abnormal reflex:
      - Absence of contraction of brachioradialis (C6)
      - Hyperactive contraction of finger flexors
  - Scapulohumeral reflex - tap on scapular spine or acromion –\> normally causes elevation of scapulae +/- abduction of humerus
- Imaging assessment:
  - X Ray
  - MRI C-spine (T2-weighted), w/ assessment of:
    - Level of lesion
    - Pathoanatomy
    - Obliteration of CSF space
    - Cord shape
    - Intramedullary signal change (myelomalacia)
  - CT - rule out other pathological processes:
    - e.g. OPLL - ossification of posterior longitudinal ligament
- Natural Hx
  - Progressive deteriorating disorder:
    - 75% patients - stepwise progression
    - 20% - slow and steady deterioration
    - 5% - rapid onset w/ stable plateau
- Indications of surgery:
  - Progressive neurological deficit
  - Significant impaired ADL
  - Compatible imaging findings
- Surgical approach - anterior vs posterior approach:
  - Pathoanatomy - e.g. disc protrusion vs flavum
  - Sagittal aligment - kyphotic deformities favours anterior approach to fix cervical kyphosis
  - Number of levels involved - \>3 levels favours posterior approach
  - Sublaxation or instability -fusion is indicated
  - Neck pain
- Complications of anterior surgery:
  - Esophageal injury
  - Vertebral artery injury
  - Airway compromise
  - Pseudoarthrosis
  - Adjacent level degeneration
- Posterior surgery:
  - Post-op kyphosis - lost stabilisation of the anterior aspect + weakening of extensor muscles
  - Axial Sx - shoulder and neck pain
  - C5 nerve root paresis - traction of C5 nerve upon extension?

## Cervical radiculopathy

- Clinical features:
  - Unilateral arm pain or sensory disturbance
  - Associated weakness over myotome
  - Dermatomal distribution
  - Neck pain
  - Absence of long-tract signs
- Signs:
  - Spuring's test - extend and lateral bend towards symptomatic side to narrow neuroforamina
  - Shoulder abduction test - abduct shoulder, relieved shoulder pain in radiculopathy, but worsen pain if shoulder pathology
  - Myotomal weakness
  - Reduced upper limb reflex
- DDx:
  - Cervical myelopathy
  - Peripheral nerve compression
  - Shoulder pathologies (esp of C5 radiculopathy)
- Ix:
  - XR - AP, lateral and oblique
  - CT/MRI
  - NCV/EMG

## Cervical trauma and spinal cord injury

- Epidemiology:
  - Bimodal distribution
    - Young adults - high energy trauma (risk of polytrauma, examine and X Ray of whole spine)
    - Old adults - low eneergy trauma, osteoporotic bone, pre-existing stenosis
- Transfer:
  - Scene to hospital, A and E to ward - continue immobilisation until clinical and radiological clearance
  - Immobilisation - spine board and log roll
- Emergency Mx:
  - ATLS for assessment and resuscitation
    - Airway maintenace and C-spine protection
    - Breathing
    - Circulation
    - Disability - motor/ sensory
    - Exposure - includes log-roll to inspect and examine spine and PR exam (check tone)
  - Trauma series - CXR, lateral C-spine, AP pelvis
  - Polytrauma CT
  - +/- MRI if suspect soft tissue injury
  - NEXUS criteria to clear cervical spine - indicate XR of spine
    - Neuro deficit
    - EtOH
    - Extreme distracting injuries
    - Unable to provide Hx
    - Spinal tenderness
  - C-Spine stabilisation - rigid collar, sand-bags and tapes, Skull traction
- Evaluation
  - Hx - type of trauma, time of injury, any delay in Dx
  - P/E:
    - Head to toe - for wounds (related to mechanism of injury)
    - Spine - bruising, tenderness, stepping, restricted movement
    - Neuro - assess level of injury, severity of neurological damage, need for intervention, and prognosis
- Spinal Shock (pathophysiological concept) - perior of temporal LOF of spine after injury (h to d):
  - LMN presentation below level of lesion + loss of bowel and bladder control
  - Over if bulbocarvenous +/- anal wink reflex resumed, only assess neuro after spinal shock over (for prognosis)
- Types of incomplete injury/ spinal cord syndromes:
  - Central cord syndrome
  - Brown sequard syndrome
  - Anterior Cord syndrome
  - Posterior cord syndrome
  - Cauda Equina syndrome
  - Mixed
- Mx of SCI:
  - Preventing secondary injury - due to delayed Mx due to further oedema or inappropriate cord Mx (e.g. inaddequate stabilisation)
    - Proper transfer
    - Steroid (NASCIS trial shows controversial results)
    - Decompression
    - STabilisation
  - Indications:
    - Structural instability

    - Decompression:w

    - Polytrauma
